<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=shaduf">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shadoof</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2200 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/23rd-century-bce">23rd century BCE</a> · Also known as shaduf, well sweep, well pole, swape</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The shadoof is a tool for lifting water from a well, river or pond onto land or into a channel: a long pole pivoted on an upright frame, with a bucket at one end and a counterweight of clay or stone at the other. The earliest evidence of it is a cylinder seal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> of about 2200 BCE; it appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> sometime after 2000 BCE and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> around the same time. One man can lift 39 to 130 litres a minute with it, and it is still used for irrigation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Asia and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaduf.jpg" alt="Shadoof" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noumenon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Well-Central_Anatolia.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 2200 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Mesopotamia</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Some historians hold that it began on the Nile, citing tomb drawings at Thebes from 1250 BCE; another theory, with little evidence, places its origin in India.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/irrigation">Irrigation<span>c. 6000 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/crane-machine">Crane<span>late 6th century BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=shaduf">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
